Exactly. The physical act can be identical; what changes is the relational layer.

“Making love” requires something beyond mechanics—care, vulnerability, mutual recognition, trust, desire directed at that person, and some sense of shared meaning. Remove that layer and you can still have pleasure, intimacy, even tenderness, but semantically it collapses toward sex.

So the distinction isn’t really in what the bodies are doing. It’s in what the act is carrying.

Sex is an action.
Making love is an action plus meaning.